  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 PM EDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nFast moving upper flow across the northern tier of the CONUS with \nPacific energy sliding over a developing ridge and into the \nGreat Lakes. Synoptically, this upcoming pattern has heavy rain \npotential signals for parts of the Great Lakes. This includes \nimpressive northward moisture transport, favorable upper level \njet energy north of a strengthening upper ridge and a stalled \nout frontal boundary over the northern Ohio Valley/southern \nLakes. A tightening pressure gradient early in the week will \ncreate windy conditions as well. \n\nSurface high pressure slides across the area tonight, setting the \narea up for very cool temperatures.  Widespread 40s over interior \nareas with typical colder locations in the mid to upper 30s (a touch \nof patchy frost?).  A few wildcards in the forecast which could \nimpact temperatures include some lingering lake cloud potential into \nthe evening and the possibility that we don't fully decouple the \nwinds until the surface high drifts more directly overhead.   \n\nWe are still looking at widespread rain potential Monday night\ninto early Tuesday with strengthening moist, southwest flow in \nadvance of a cold front. Models have backed off a little on the \nbroader heavy rain signals for northern Michigan, placing the \nbest f-gen response and moisture transport primarily to our west\nMonday night and to our south on Tuesday. Prob QPF numbers \nstill show some potential for 1\"+ rainfall amounts, although \nthose numbers have trended downward in recent runs. The pattern \nis fairly progressive, as the shortwave energy shears out over \nOntario and the front slides across the area by later Tuesday. \nWinds will certainly ramp up Monday night into early Tuesday \nwith a 40+ knot low level jet in the weakening phase as it \npushes toward our area. Not the most favorable pattern to fully \nmix these winds to the surface given continued warm air \nadvection. Despite that, think some areas closer to Lake \nMichigan could see 40+ mph gusts, lower inland. \n\nSome question marks for the middle/end of the week as additional \nshortwave energy rides along a quasi-stationary boundary to our \nsouth.  Best rain potential will be south, closer to the boundary \nbut at least some shower chances for the second half of the week \nover northern Michigan.  A stronger shortwave may move toward the \nGreat Lakes next weekend, bringing better chances of widespread \nrain. Temperatures generally fairly close to normal for mid \nSeptember, with highs in the upper 60s to lower 70s and lows in the \n40s to mid 50s.",
